22:25:20,420 root DEBUG {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You will be given a SQL query and some strategies to rewrite the given SQL query. Your task is to summarize the provided query rewrite strategies into one paragraph.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\nSQL Query:\n```sql\nselect  count(*) from (\n    select distinct c_last_name, c_first_name, d_date\n    from store_sales, date_dim, customer\n          where store_sales.ss_sold_date_sk = date_dim.d_date_sk\n      and store_sales.ss_customer_sk = customer.c_customer_sk\n      and d_month_seq between 1221 and 1221 + 11\n      and c_birth_month in (2, 8, 10, 11)\n      and ss_list_price between 241 and 300\n      and ss_wholesale_cost BETWEEN 73 AND 93\n  intersect\n    select distinct c_last_name, c_first_name, d_date\n    from catalog_sales, date_dim, customer\n          where catalog_sales.cs_sold_date_sk = date_dim.d_date_sk\n      and catalog_sales.cs_bill_customer_sk = customer.c_customer_sk\n      and d_month_seq between 1221 and 1221 + 11\n      and c_birth_month in (2, 8, 10, 11)\n      and cs_list_price between 241 and 300\n      and cs_wholesale_cost BETWEEN 73 AND 93\n  intersect\n    select distinct c_last_name, c_first_name, d_date\n    from web_sales, date_dim, customer\n          where web_sales.ws_sold_date_sk = date_dim.d_date_sk\n      and web_sales.ws_bill_customer_sk = customer.c_customer_sk\n      and d_month_seq between 1221 and 1221 + 11\n      and c_birth_month in (2, 8, 10, 11)\n      and ws_list_price between 241 and 300\n      and ws_wholesale_cost BETWEEN 73 AND 93\n) hot_cust\nlimit 100;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Strategies:\nQuery Rewrite 1:\n"""The query rewrite process involves transforming the original query into a more efficient form by moving certain filter conditions from the WHERE clause to the ON clause or as a subquery in the respective side of the join. This is done to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs, which can improve query performance.\n\nFor example, the original query has three INTERSECT operations that combine data from different tables (store_sales, catalog_sales, and web_sales). The query rewrite rule applies Case 1 by moving certain filter conditions from the WHERE clause to the ON clause or as a subquery in the respective side of the join. This is done to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs, which can improve query performance.\n\nThe rewritten query has three INNER JOIN operations that combine data from different tables (store_sales, catalog_sales, and web_sales). The filter conditions are applied to each table separately before the join occurs, which can reduce the size of the resulting dataset and improve query performance."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""Explaining the SQL query changes based on the matched case:\n\nThe query rewrite rule applies Case 1 to the given SQL query. This means that the original query is transformed by directly applying the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before projection. The outer projection layer is eliminated if it only renames the columns without transforming them.\n\nThe rewritten query has a single `LogicalIntersect` operation, which replaces the original three `INTERSECT` operations. The filter predicates and join conditions have been modified to reflect the changes made by the rewrite rule."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""The query rewrite process applies the simplifications to the original SQL query. The rewritten query has simplified conditions in the WHERE clause that are more efficient and easier to evaluate."""'}], 'response': 'Here is a summary of the provided query rewrite strategies:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using three different strategies. Query Rewrite 1 involves transforming the original query into a more efficient form by moving certain filter conditions from the WHERE clause to the ON clause or as a subquery in the respective side of the join, reducing the size of datasets before the join occurs and improving query performance. Query Rewrite 2 applies Case 1 by directly applying aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the FROM clause of the inner query before projection, eliminating the outer projection layer if it only renames columns without transforming them. Query Rewrite 3 simplifies conditions in the WHERE clause, making them more efficient and easier to evaluate.', 'time': 1.5189027786254883}
